// Book Three, Chapter 9 (session 33). The end of August 1789. The Declaration of the Rights of Man, the Cadets, the boy, the Prince's leave, and Galerius's last night in the ossuary.
// Opens on the shop on the twenty-sixth (no phase), then one night phase of three actions out of four (the night of the thirtieth), then a fixed closing scene (the hunt), then the dawn of the thirty first.
// Ends with rest() and => next. Reads b3.greve, b3.lictor, b3.cadets, b3.abolition, b3.sugar, b3.presses_use, b3.galerius, b3.file, b3.severin and b3.veil. Chapter 10 should read hunger, standing and secrecy as this chapter left them.
@chapter b3c09
@title The Thirty-First
@book crown-of-ashes
@version 1

# start
## The twenty-sixth
$ b3.rights = "refused"
$ b3.exodus = "stood"
$ b3.boy = "left"
$ b3.protest = "left"
$ b3.hunt = "taken"
~> shop
?if must_feed()
  ~> hunger
?end
---
$ do phase("night", 3)
It is the night of the thirtieth of August. You have a night and a part of another, and four things that want doing by your own reckoning, and time for three. There is a fifth, which has no choice in it, and which is waiting for the end of the night in the place that it has picked.
-> night

# shop
It is the twenty-sixth, a Wednesday, and Baptiste has been at the Assembly since seven in the morning, in the gallery, with a pencil and a hat full of sandwiches, and he comes in at nine in the evening with his collar gone and his face shining. It has been passed. It has been debated for a week, article by article, under a very high ceiling, by men who sat up with candles and argued over every comma, and what they have made out of it is a single sheet. It is seventeen articles. It is, says Baptiste, standing in the middle of the floor with the paper held out in front of him in both hands like a dish, the shortest thing that has ever been written that is going to take a hundred years to read.
He reads it aloud. He reads it twice. Men are born and remain free and equal in rights. Liberty consists in the power to do whatever does not injure another. The law is the expression of the general will. The Valère shop has heard a good many things read aloud in its time, by candle, in a low voice, with the shutters closed, and it has never heard anything like the silence in which the old man at the second press stands with a stick of type in his hand and does not move.
Étienne asks to see it. He takes his spectacles from his pocket, and reads it again to himself, slowly, to the end. Then he says, quietly, that it says nothing whatever about the people in the inventory of a plantation. He says it without heat. He says that it will be a very curious thing to see how long a sentence can go on being true for one half of a street.
?if b3.greve == "stopped"
  Thibault has been in the back of the shop for three weeks, on a stool by the stove, with a duster, because he cannot think of anything else that he is permitted to hold. He has not said very much. He has listened to Baptiste, with his mouth a little open, and when the line came about the law being the same for all, whether it protects or punishes, he put the duster down on his knee.
?elif b3.greve == "allowed"
  Thibault has not been to the shop since the Place de Grève. There is a lilac ribbon on the doorpost that nobody has put there, and it has been there for a week.
?else
  Nobody has seen Thibault. It is understood, in the way that things are understood on that stair, that it is better not to ask.
?end
Adélaïde has put the lamp on the corner of the counter and is looking at a calendar that hangs on the wall behind it, a printer's calendar, with the days struck through in red. She has not looked at it for a month. She turns her head and meets your eye. There are five days left in it.
?if b3.lictor == "gave"
  You gave a name. You know it, as one knows a loose tooth, with the tip of the tongue.
?elif b3.lictor == "bargained"
  You have put your own name in a very old book. It is a thing that is not easy to forget at a printer's, where the whole world is put in the way of a signature.
?else
  You told a gray old man that you did not know, and he is an old man who has not been lied to by anybody that he did not remember.
?end
<~

# hunger
## The sticky heat
It is the end of the heat, and it has left the city sour. There is a sweetish smell of cabbage and wet slate, and under it, running along the walls of the houses like a trail of ink, the long warm smell of a great many people asleep with their windows open. There is a boy of twelve in the gutter below the shop with a tray of lemons. He has fallen asleep with his head on the tray.
* Take what you need from a sleeping drunk in the rue de la Harpe
  $ do feed("human")
  $ humanity -= 2
  $ cunning_brutal += 1
  He is a man of the usual sort. He has a purse, which you leave, and a snore that does not alter. It is done in a doorway in perhaps a minute. You lay him on his side, as before, and put his hat over his face.
* Go down to the stable of the Hôtel de Cluny and feed on a carthorse
  $ do feed("animal")
  $ humanity += 1
  $ secrecy += 1
  It is a very large gray, with feet like buckets, and it does not object. It is a quiet and a heavy business. You lean your forehead on the great warm shoulder when it is over, for a moment, and it blows through its nose, comfortably, as at a friend.
* Put it off, and go on
  $ cunning_brutal += 2
  $ humanity += 1
  It is a thing that can be put off. It will not be put off for very long. You can feel it at the back of the neck, politely, like a clerk who is waiting for a signature.
