# dawn
## The thirty-first
It is a gray, cold, windless morning. The first carts are in the street. There is a smell of baking, and the sound of the shutters of the rue Saint-Jacques going up one after another, with a clatter, like a long line of men clearing their throats.
?if b3.hunt == "destroyed"
  You walk home along the river. It is not a thing that you remember very well. There is a bakery at the corner of the rue de la Huchette, and the girl at the door says good morning, and you say it back, and she gives you, without being asked, a small, hot, floury roll, in a twist of paper. You hold it for a long time. You do not eat it.
?elif b3.hunt == "spared"
  You walk home along the river. There is a gray wind, from the north, with the smell of the snow in it. It is the first morning in a month on which you have not felt the Senate on the back of your neck. You are aware that it will come back.
?elif b3.hunt == "sworn"
  You walk home along the river, with a very old promise round your neck like a stone on a string. It is perfectly bearable. It is something that you will feel for a long time, in a certain kind of weather.
?else
  You walk home along the river, in the cold, with nothing in your hands. It is the thirty-first of August. A form has been filled in, and it has your name on one line of it, in a close tiny script, and it is a very clean piece of work.
?end
?if b3.boy == "taught"
  Thibault is waiting on the step of the shop, with his hand flat against the doorpost. He looks up at you, and then, with extreme care, takes it away.
?elif b3.boy == "hidden"
  There is a letter on the counter, in a bookbinder's neat commercial hand, from Lyon. It says, in four lines, that the parcel has arrived, and that it is very quiet, and that it eats very little.
?end
?if b3.rights != "refused"
  There is a bundle of the Declaration on the counter, with the string cut. The first of the sheets has already been pinned to the wall of the shop, over the stove, by Adélaïde, with four pins. It has been put a little crooked. She has not straightened it.
?end
Étienne comes down in his shirtsleeves to say that the Assembly has been arguing all night about whether the king shall have a veto. He says that it will be a long autumn.
$ do rest()
